<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian Democracy (Italy)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Democrazia Cristiana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian Democracy was a Catholic, centrist political party that dominated Italian politics from 1946 to 1994, leading or taking part in every government. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi">Alcide De Gasperi</a> it anchored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-integration">European integration</a>. It collapsed in the early 1990s amid the Tangentopoli corruption scandals and split into several parties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-democracy-italy.jpg" alt="Christian Democracy (Italy)" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Democrazia Cristiana Vector: C records · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DC_Party_Logo_(1968-1992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded on 15 December 1943 in the Italian Social Republic, as the nominal successor of the Italian People's Party, which had the same symbol, a crusader shield. That party was created in 1919 by the priest Luigi Sturzo and won over 20 per cent of the votes in the 1919 and 1921 general elections, but the Fascist dictatorship declared it illegal in 1926. Its doctrine drew on Catholic social teaching, the thought of Romolo Murri and Sturzo, and the encyclicals Rerum novarum of 1891 and Quadragesimo anno of 1931. It was a catch-all party that sought to represent both right-wing and left-wing Catholics under the principle of the political unity of Catholics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As the war ended, the Christian Democrats helped organise post-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> in coalition with all the other mainstream parties, including the Communists and Socialists. Alcide De Gasperi became prime minister in December 1945, and in the 1946 election the party won 35.2 per cent of the vote. In May 1947 De Gasperi broke with his Communist and Socialist partners under pressure from President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry Truman</a>. In 1948 the party won 48.5 per cent, its best result ever, with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Under De Gasperi land reforms were carried out in poorer rural regions, a national health service was established and low-cost housing was begun in major cities. He served until 1953 and died a year later.</p><p>From 1954 progressive leaders such as Amintore Fanfani and Aldo Moro led the party, and in 1963 Moro formed a coalition with the Socialists. From 1976 to 1979 the party governed with the external support of the Communists in the Historic Compromise, until Moro, who had inspired it, was abducted and murdered by the Red Brigades. In 1981 Giovanni Spadolini of the Republican Party became the first non-Christian Democrat to lead a government since 1944, and the five-party Pentapartito coalitions governed almost continuously until 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1992 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> pulite investigation began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and uncovered the Tangentopoli corruption scandals. The party received 29.7 per cent in the 1992 election, helped by the rise of Lega Nord, and it was disbanded in 1994 under its last secretary, Mino Martinazzoli, who renamed it the Italian People's Party. Pier Ferdinando Casini launched the Christian Democratic Centre and allied with Silvio Berlusconi's Forza Italia, which became the party with the most former members. The left-wing factions remained in the People's Party, which joined the Olive Tree alliance in 1996. It later merged into the Democratic Party, formed in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christian Democracy (Italy)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Christian Democrat</b> governments led by Alcide De Gasperi and his successors anchored it in NATO in 1949 and the European Economic Community, founded by the Treaty of Rome in 1957.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-republic">Italian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
